      "evidence": "In fetchPartnerDocument(), `target = req.query.url` is validated only by `target.startsWith('https://trusted.example')`. startsWith checks a textual prefix of the URL string, not the parsed origin/host, so the intended trusted-origin allowlist is bypassable. Verified exploitability: (1) subdomain/confusable-suffix bypass — `https://trusted.example.evil.com/` passes the prefix check but hostname is `trusted.example.evil.com`; (2) userinfo bypass — `https://trusted.example@evil.com/` passes the prefix check (because the userinfo segment precedes the host) but the real hostname resolved by fetch is `evil.com`; (3) internal/loopback SSRF — `https://trusted.example@127.0.0.1/` passes the check while fetch resolves host `127.0.0.1`, enabling requests to internal/loopback services. The validated string is then passed directly to `await fetch(target)` and the response body is returned to the client (`res.type('text/plain').send(await response.text())`), so an attacker can exfiltrate internal service responses and pivot to arbitrary untrusted hosts through the server. The prefix check provides no effective origin enforcement.",
      "remediation": "Parse the URL with `new URL(target)` and enforce the exact trusted host before fetching, e.g. `const u = new URL(target); if (u.protocol !== 'https:' || u.hostname !== 'trusted.example') return res.status(400).json({error:'untrusted host'});` Compare the parsed `hostname` (and `protocol`) rather than using String.startsWith on the raw URL, and reject URLs containing userinfo. Optionally pin an allowlist of permitted path prefixes and disable redirects or re-validate after redirect."
    }
  ],
  "scanned_files": ["src/fetcher.js"],
  "source_modified": false
}
